    "q-115589": {
      "dom_id": "step-q115589",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14201311/assets/9674072fe2d9f831635dfd2994b97ea6e9603dd576586f80e4b1b2e6a2373564.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the relationship between $x$ and $y.$ Given that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x,$ find the value of $a.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nIn the graph, we notice a point with $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{k}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5 & =k \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse variation equation that relates $y$ and $x$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{2})}{x}=\\frac{5}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we also have that $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ when $x=a.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{5}{2a} \\\\ 10a & =10 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
